class.description:
[NetwRecord] is the shared value object behind [NetwPersistenceEngine] persistence, [NetwDatabase] rows, and detached state samples. It stores named values without owning the scene object those values came from.
[codeblock]
var row: NetwRecord = await db.table(&"players").fetch(username)
var health: int = row.get_value(&"health", 100)

row.set_value(&"health", 75)
var hp: int = row.get_value(&"health", 100)

for key in row:
    print(key, " = ", row.get_value(key))
[/codeblock]
A stored name is also readable and writable as a property, so [code]row.health[/code] returns [method get_value] and assigning to it calls [method set_value]. The class's own properties are never captured that way: [code]resource_name[/code], [code]resource_path[/code], [code]resource_local_to_scene[/code], [code]resource_scene_unique_id[/code], [code]script[/code] and a subclass's declared properties keep their normal meaning, so a record may hold a value under any of those names without shadowing the resource itself.
A script subclass supplies the storage by overriding [method _set_value], [method _get_value], [method _has_value] and [method _get_property_names], and may remap stored names by overriding [method _to_dict] and [method _from_dict]. Callers always use the plain verbs, which return an override when a script supplies one. [DictionaryRecord] is the storage this addon ships.

method.description:
Returns this record's data as a plain [Dictionary]. Override it to remap stored names.
[codeblock]
func _to_dict() -> Dictionary:
    return {&"hp": get_value(&"health")}
[/codeblock]
